INT. FUNERAL PARLOR - VIEWING ROOM - NIGHT

Carpet muffles footfalls. Lilies in tall vases. A closed casket on a draped stand. A single lamp.

DETECTIVE MARISOL OKAFOR-DUMAS, 40s, in a black blazer, stands near the casket, hands behind her back. She watches the door. MR. LEOPOLD GRIMSBY, 60s, the funeral director, stands rigid in a dark suit. A mourner, PHILOMENA TEAGUE-ABARA, 50s, sits in the front row, handkerchief twisted in her fist. In the back, a young usher named CALEB NASH, 20s, fidgets with his cufflinks.

LEOPOLD
The family asked for privacy, Detective.

MARISOL
I won't be long.

PHILOMENA
(without turning)
She was my sister.

MARISOL
I know.

PHILOMENA
Then you know she wasn't well.

MARISOL
I know she was poisoned.

Caleb drops a cufflink. It rolls under a pew. He doesn't pick it up.

LEOPOLD
That is a serious accusation.

MARISOL
It's a finding.

PHILOMENA
By whom?

MARISOL
The lab. It came back this afternoon.

Philomena finally turns. Her eyes are dry. Her mouth is steady.

PHILOMENA
And who do you think did it?

MARISOL
Someone who had access to her tea.

Leopold shifts his weight. Caleb takes a step toward the exit. Marisol raises one hand.

MARISOL (CONT'D)
Mr. Nash. Stay.

CALEB
I was just getting air.

MARISOL
The air is fine here.

The lamp flickers. Marisol steps to the casket and lays a hand on the lid.

MARISOL (CONT'D)
Mrs. Teague-Abara. Tell me about the tea.

PHILOMENA
It was chamomile.

MARISOL
Who bought it?

PHILOMENA
I did.

MARISOL
Who poured it?

PHILOMENA
I did.

A pause. The lilies sway. Caleb breathes out.

MARISOL
Thank you.

PHILOMENA
Is that all?

MARISOL
For tonight.

She nods to Leopold, then to Caleb. Philomena rises, smooths her skirt, and walks out. The door sighs shut behind her. Marisol lifts the lid of the casket just a crack and looks at something inside.
